# CANONICAL earcut.hpp v2.2.4 SPECIFICATION (merged)

**Provenance note (important for trust weighting):** `/workspace/dataset/test4.cpp` and `/workspace/dataset/test4_executable` do exist on disk. The driver builds `n` rings, ring `i` = `[(2i,2i),(2i+n,2i),(2i+n,2i+n),(2i,2i+n)]` (CCW, side `n`, diagonal offset 2), ring 0 = outer, rings 1..n-1 = holes, and calls `mapbox::earcut<uint32_t>`. So Report 5's oracle outputs are real, and its variant verdicts (read from machine code) outrank the other four reports' recollections wherever they conflict — **except** where a claim contradicts three independent reports *and* is FP-neutral for this input (see Disagreement D2).

Derived facts for this input: total verts = `4n`; hashing turns on at **n ≥ 21**; every hole's leftmost vertex is `(2i,2i)` so all sort keys are distinct; all coordinates are small integers, so every product/difference in every predicate is **computed exactly** in `double` (no rounding at all until `n` gets large). That last fact demotes every "expression rearrangement" risk to near-zero for *this* input and promotes every *structural / comparison-operator* risk.

---

## PART 0 — Types and functor state

```
struct Node {
    const N      i;        // vertex index into flattened concatenation of all rings
    const double x, y;     // ALWAYS double, even for integer/float input points
    Node*        prev = nullptr;
    Node*        next = nullptr;
    int32_t      z    = 0;        // z-order (Morton) key
    Node*        prevZ = nullptr;
    Node*        nextZ = nullptr;
    bool         steiner = false;
};
// Rust: arena `Vec<Node>` + `u32`/`usize` handles. Every `p != q` in this spec is
// HANDLE identity, never coordinate comparison. Every `p->i != q->i` is INDEX
// comparison and is NOT the same thing (splitPolygon mints duplicate-index nodes).

struct Earcut<N> {
    std::vector<N>     indices;      // OUTPUT
    std::size_t        vertices = 0; // running base index; reset per call
    ObjectStore<Node>  nodes;        // arena; nodes are NEVER freed mid-run
    std::vector<Node*> queue;        // hole queue (member; cleared at entry — a local
                                     //   vector is behaviorally identical)
    bool   hashing;
    double minX, maxX, minY, maxY, inv_size;
};
```

Sign convention, load-bearing everywhere: `area()` is the **negative** of the usual CCW cross product. **Negative area == convex/CCW turn.** `area(...) >= 0` means reflex-or-collinear.

---

## PART 1 — Every function, in dependency order

### 1.1 `area`

```
double area(const Node* p, const Node* q, const Node* r) {
    return (q->y - p->y) * (r->x - q->x) - (q->x - p->x) * (r->y - q->y);
}
```
Verbatim. No rearrangement, no FMA (`-ffp-contract=off`), no x87 excess precision, no `-ffast-math`.

### 1.2 `equals`

```
bool equals(const Node* p1, const Node* p2) {
    return p1->x == p2->x && p1->y == p2->y;   // exact ==, no epsilon anywhere in earcut
}
```

### 1.3 `pointInTriangle`  *(see Disagreement D2)*

```
bool pointInTriangle(double ax, double ay, double bx, double by,
                     double cx, double cy, double px, double py) {
    return (cx - px) * (ay - py) - (ax - px) * (cy - py) >= 0 &&
           (ax - px) * (by - py) - (bx - px) * (ay - py) >= 0 &&
           (bx - px) * (cy - py) - (cx - px) * (by - py) >= 0;
}
```
Three cross products in the pair order (C,A), (A,B), (B,C); all three `>= 0` (boundary counts as inside); `&&` short-circuits left-to-right. Do **not** implement JS-3.0's `pointInTriangleExceptFirst`.

### 1.4 `sign`

```
int sign(double val) { return (0.0 < val) - (val < 0.0); }   // NaN -> 0
```

### 1.5 `onSegment`

```
bool onSegment(const Node* p, const Node* q, const Node* r) {   // q inside bbox(p,r)
    return q->x <= std::max<double>(p->x, r->x) &&
           q->x >= std::min<double>(p->x, r->x) &&
           q->y <= std::max<double>(p->y, r->y) &&
           q->y >= std::min<double>(p->y, r->y);
}
```

### 1.6 `intersects`

```
bool intersects(const Node* p1, const Node* q1, const Node* p2, const Node* q2) {
    int o1 = sign(area(p1, q1, p2));
    int o2 = sign(area(p1, q1, q2));
    int o3 = sign(area(p2, q2, p1));
    int o4 = sign(area(p2, q2, q1));

    if (o1 != o2 && o3 != o4) return true;                 // general case
    if (o1 == 0 && onSegment(p1, p2, q1)) return true;
    if (o2 == 0 && onSegment(p1, q2, q1)) return true;
    if (o3 == 0 && onSegment(p2, p1, q2)) return true;
    if (o4 == 0 && onSegment(p2, q1, q2)) return true;
    return false;
}
```
All four orientations computed up front (no short-circuit). This version returns **true** for touching/collinear-overlapping segments — intentional; callers filter by index.

### 1.7 `intersectsPolygon`

```
bool intersectsPolygon(const Node* a, const Node* b) {
    const Node* p = a;
    do {
        if (p->i != a->i && p->next->i != a->i && p->i != b->i && p->next->i != b->i &&
            intersects(p, p->next, a, b)) return true;
        p = p->next;
    } while (p != a);
    return false;
}
```
Forward from `a`. Guards compare `->i`, **not** pointers. `intersects` arg order is `(p, p->next, a, b)`.

### 1.8 `locallyInside`

```
bool locallyInside(const Node* a, const Node* b) {
    return area(a->prev, a, a->next) < 0 ?
        (area(a, b, a->next) >= 0 && area(a, a->prev, b) >= 0) :
        (area(a, b, a->prev) < 0  || area(a, a->next, b) < 0);
}
```
Convex branch is `>= 0 && >= 0`; reflex branch is `< 0 || < 0`. Discriminant strict `< 0` (collinear `a` → reflex branch). The asymmetry is deliberate.

### 1.9 `middleInside`

```
bool middleInside(const Node* a, const Node* b) {
    const Node* p = a;
    bool inside = false;
    double px = (a->x + b->x) / 2;
    double py = (a->y + b->y) / 2;
    do {
        if (((p->y > py) != (p->next->y > py)) && p->next->y != p->y &&
