## PART 3 — RANKED uncertainty switches
*(Ranked by likelihood of mattering for N heavily-overlapping axis-aligned squares, all rings after the first treated as holes. Flip these one at a time in a differential test.)*

**#1 — `eliminateHole` return/filter form.**
 A: (2.2.4) `bridge=findHoleBridge(...); if(!bridge) return outerNode; brv=splitPolygon(bridge,hole); filterPoints(brv,brv->next); return filterPoints(bridge,bridge->next);`
 B: (≤2.2.2) void form; `filterPoints(bridge,…)` first, `filterPoints(brv,…)` second; caller loop does `outerNode = filterPoints(outerNode, outerNode->next);` after each hole.
 *Why #1:* every hole is bridged into a ring that already contains all previously merged holes, so the anchor returned here becomes the *scan origin* for the next `findHoleBridge`, whose `x > qx` is strict. One divergence cascades into a totally different triangle set from hole 1 onward.

**#2 — `findHoleBridge` early return `if (x == hx) return m;`.**
 A: present, inside the `x <= hx && x > qx` block, after `qx = x` and after `m = …`.
 B: absent (loop always completes; ties resolved only by `x > qx`), optionally with the 2.1.x `if (hx == qx) return m->prev;` after the loop.
 *Why #2:* hole `i`'s leftmost x is `2i`; the merged ring carries vertical edges at `x ∈ {0, n, 2j, 2j+n}`. `x == hx` becomes reachable exactly when `2j + n == 2i`, i.e. **only for even n**. This is almost certainly the mechanism behind the odd/even bifurcation in the oracle outputs. Getting it wrong flips roughly half the test values.

**#3 — `isValidDiagonal` extra clauses.**
 A: `A && B && C && ((locallyInside∧locallyInside∧middleInside ∧ (area(a->prev,a,b->prev)!=0 || area(a,b->prev,b)!=0)) || (equals(a,b) ∧ area(a->prev,a,a->next)>0 ∧ area(b->prev,b,b->next)>0))`.
 B: flat `a->next->i!=b->i && a->prev->i!=b->i && !intersectsPolygon && locallyInside(a,b) && locallyInside(b,a) && middleInside(a,b)`.
 *Why #3:* `splitEarcut` definitely fires on this input (n=4 emits only 6 triangles from a 22-node ring). Axis-aligned overlap ⇒ exactly-zero areas everywhere ⇒ the sectors clause rejects diagonals that B accepts ⇒ different split ⇒ different remainder of the index stream.

**#4 — `intersects` robust vs. legacy.**
 A: `sign`/`onSegment` orientation form (o1..o4, general case, four collinear cases).
 B: `if ((equals(p1,q1)&&equals(p2,q2)) || (equals(p1,q2)&&equals(p2,q1))) return true; return (area(p1,q1,p2)>0)!=(area(p1,q1,q2)>0) && (area(p2,q2,p1)>0)!=(area(p2,q2,q1)>0);`
 *Why #4:* B returns **false** for collinear-overlapping and endpoint-touching pairs, which is the dominant configuration here. Feeds both `cureLocalIntersections` (pass 1, which does fire) and `intersectsPolygon` (pass 2).

**#5 — `sectorContainsSector` tie-break arm.**
 A: ungated `(p->x > m->x || sectorContainsSector(m, p))`.
 B: gated `(p->x > m->x || (p->x == m->x && sectorContainsSector(m, p)))`.
 C: absent entirely (2.1.x): `(tan == tanMin && p->x > m->x)`.
 *Why #5:* every candidate with `p->y == hy` gives `tan == 0.0` **exactly**, so the tie-break runs on essentially every bridge search.

**#6 — `findHoleBridge` loop-2 start and frozen-vs-live `m`.**
 A: `p = m;` do/while; gate uses frozen `mx`; tie-break uses live `m->x`.
 B: `p = m->next;` while; both frozen (or both live).
 *Why #6:* changes whether `m` seeds `tanMin = 0` on iteration 0, which changes every subsequent `tan == tanMin` comparison.

**#7 — `earcutLinked` ear-advance / stop-reset.**
 A: on a successful clip, `ear = stop = next->next` (skip one vertex), then `continue`.
 B: `ear = next` (no skip) and/or `stop` left alone.
 *Why #7:* no version variance found, so low *risk*, but it is the single biggest determinant of emission order — a transcription slip here changes everything. Verify against `n=1 → "2 3 0 0 1 2"` immediately (see Part 5 trace).

**#8 — bbox computed over the merged ring (post-`eliminateHoles`) vs. over ring 0 only.**
 A: walk the linked list from `outerNode` after hole merging (includes hole vertices and bridge duplicates).
 B: JS-style, over the raw `points[0]` slice.
 *Why #8:* only active at **n ≥ 21** (hashing on), but there it changes `inv_size` → every `z` → the sorted z-chain → `isEarHashed` scan order. The oracle has n=21 and n=40 values to test against.

**#9 — `zOrder` reciprocal-multiply vs. divide.**
 A: `inv_size = 32767. / span;` then `(v - min) * inv_size`.
 B: `32767. * (v - min) / span`.
 *Why #9:* n ≥ 21 only; one rounding vs. two, which can move a coordinate into a different 15-bit bucket and reorder the z-chain.

**#10 — hashing threshold semantics.**
 A: `int threshold = 80;` decremented per ring while `threshold >= 0`; `hashing = threshold < 0`. (Exactly 80 total verts ⇒ **off**.)
 B: `total > 80` computed over all rings.
 *Why #10:* they differ only in the short-circuit's effect on `len` (allocation), not on `hashing`, for uniform ring sizes — but the boundary is exactly n=20 (off) / n=21 (on) here, so a `>=` slip flips n=20.

**#11 — `linkedList` winding test and reverse-loop index assignment.**
 A: `clockwise == (sum > 0)`; reverse loop `for (i = len; i-- > 0;) insertNode(vertices + i, points[i], last)` — index by **input position**.
 B: strict/non-strict slip, or indices assigned by insertion order in the reverse loop.
 *Why #11:* B shifts every emitted index for every hole. Deterministically wrong, easy to self-check (`sum = 2n² > 0` for all rings here ⇒ outer forward, holes reversed).

**#12 — `isEar` bbox pre-filter (D1).** A: absent. B: present. **Provably output-neutral** — include only to match source shape.

**#13 — `pointInTriangle` subtracted vs. product-compare (D2).** A: `… - … >= 0`. B: `… >= …`. Neutral for integer coordinates; keep A.

**#14 — `isEarHashed` loop shape (D3).** A: bidirectional + 2 tails. B: two sequential loops. **Output-neutral.**

**#15 — `getLeftmost` y tie-break.** A: `p->x < lm->x || (p->x == lm->x && p->y < lm->y)`. B: x only. *No-op for this input* (the `(2i,2i)` corner is already the reversed-ring traversal start and wins either way) — but implement A.

**#16 — hole-queue comparator / sort stability (D8).** A: `a->x < b->x` with unstable `std::sort`. B: `compareXYSlope`, or `std::stable_sort`. *Latent here* (all hole x distinct). Do **not** "fix" it to a stable sort.

**#17 — `cureLocalIntersections` tail.** A: `return filterPoints(p);` B: `return p;`. Fires on pass 1 for this input.

**#18 — `filterPoints` null guard / `nodes.reset` sizing / `queue` locality / declaration placement.** All output-neutral.

